[Line "3...e6 4.Nc3 Bb4 5.Bf4 Ne7 6.Qd3 b6 7.Nge2 Ba6"]
[Section "3...e6"]
[Summary "Black builds a French-style position and pins the knight. White develops with Bf4 and Qd3, aiming at e5 and queenside castling while Black tries to free his bad bishop."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e6 { [%cal Rc8f5] The most common reply in elite games (about a third). It does not
take on e4, so White has played f3 for a move that does little, and the position is a French Defence in
which the c8 bishop is buried behind ...e6 and waits for a square. The f3 pawn is no longer a recapturing
pawn: it will go to f4 after e5, which is the usual plan. } 4. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4,Gc3d5] The main move (about
two thirds of the games): the knight adds a defender to e4 and a second attacker on d5. Black's choices are
4...Bb4 (the pin), 4...Nf6 (the next game), 4...Qb6 and 4...Nd7. } 4... Bb4 { [%cal Rb4c3] Pins the knight and
takes the pressure off e4. Almost half of the games. }
( 4... dxe4 { Rare in elite play. } 5. fxe4 e5 { Black plays ...e5 anyway, two moves later than in the main
line. } 6. Nf3 { The centre is still White's, the knight is on f3 at last and Black has no developed piece. } )
( 4... Qb6 { The queen hits b2 and d4. } 5. a3 { By far the most played reply (168 elite games), but the
sample is a warning: 5...Nf6 6.Be3 won for White 16 times in 26 games, while 5...Nd7 won for Black 41 times
in 59. If Black finds ...Nd7 you are not getting an advantage. } 5... Nf6 6. Be3 )
5. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4c7,Gf4b8,Gd1d3] The study's choice, in about 12% of elite games: the bishop eyes c7 and
the b8-h2 diagonal and gets out before ...Nf6-e4 or ...Bxc3 bxc3 spoils the structure. The more common
alternatives are 5.Bd2 (a third of the games) and 5.a3 (a quarter), which both score around 54% for White. }
5... Ne7 { [%cal Re7f5] The knight heads for f5 or g6 to challenge the f4 bishop. } 6. Qd3 { [%cal Gd3c3,Gd3b5]
Backs up the pinned knight (so that ...Bxc3+ can be recaptured with the queen), eyes the b1-h7 diagonal and
leaves d1 for the rook. } 6... b6 { [%cal Rc8a6] Prepares ...Ba6 to hit the queen and bring the bishop out.
Black's whole problem in this line is the c8 bishop, and the b6-a6 plan solves it. } 7. Nge2
{ [%cal Gg1e2,Ge2c3] Develops the last piece so that ...Bxc3 can be met by Nxc3 and the structure stays
healthy, and clears the way for long castling. } 7... Ba6 { **Verdict:** balanced. Black has freed his bishop;
White has the centre and the queen has to step aside from d3. Aim for e5 followed by f4 once Black's pieces
are committed, and castle long. } *

[Line "3...e6 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.e5 Nfd7 6.f4 c5 7.Nf3 Nc6 8.Be3: a Steinitz French"]
[Section "3...e6"]
[Summary "After 4...Nf6 5.e5 the Fantasy turns into the Steinitz Variation of the French Defence, with an extra move for each side. The chain e5-f4 gives White space and a kingside attack."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 { [%cal Rf6e4] Challenges e4 at once. } 5. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6,Gf3f4] Gains a
tempo on the knight and prepares f4: the f3 pawn becomes the support of the e5 pawn, a better use of it
than in any other line. } 5... Nfd7 6. f4 { [%cal Gf3f4] White now has the Steinitz chain e5-f4. The study
says Black is "down in development" here; in fact each side has used one extra pawn move (f2-f3-f4
against c7-c6-c5), so the position is the French Steinitz Variation with the same player to move. } 6... c5
{ [%cal Rc5d4] The French break against the base of the chain. } 7. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3d4] Develops and guards d4. }
( 7. dxc5 { Gives up the tension. Black regains the pawn at once with ...Nxc5 or ...Bxc5, and the chain
loses its point. } 7... Nxc5 )
7... Nc6 { Black adds a second attacker to d4. } ( 7... cxd4 { The study's move order. } 8. Nxd4 { The exchange
on d4 hands Black the c-file, but gives White an outpost and quick development. } 8... Nc6 )
8. Be3 { [%cal Ge3d4,Gd1d2] **Position:** the main-line position of the French Steinitz (it arises in some
15,000 elite games). White supports d4 with the bishop and Qd2, castles long or short, and keeps a space
advantage.

**Plans for White:** Qd2 and O-O-O (or Be2 and O-O), then g4 and f5 or a kingside storm; keep d4 supported
and answer ...cxd4 with Nxd4. **Plans for Black:** pressure on d4 with ...cxd4 Nxd4 and ...Qb6 (or ...Bc5,
...O-O); ...f6 to undermine the e5 pawn; ...a6, ...Be7 and ...Rb8 on the queenside.

**Verdict:** equal with accurate play; the main French theory applies. The Fantasy move order suits
players who like these positions and know the French well, and it is no better than a normal French for
White. } *

[Line "4.Be3: an alternative fourth move"]
[Section "3...e6"]
[Summary "The bishop move keeps the c3 square free and meets 4...Nf6 5.e5 with the same Steinitz set-up. 4...dxe4 is the sharp reply."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e6 4. Be3 { [%cal Ge3c5] The second most popular fourth move (15%): the bishop supports d4
and keeps c3 free, so ...Bb4 has no pin. The price is that ...Qb6 hits b2 and the bishop is exposed to
...Nf6-g4 ideas. } 4... Nf6
( 4... dxe4 { The sharp reply. } 5. fxe4 Qh4+ { Black gets in the check at once. } 6. g3 Qxe4 7. Qe2
{ [%cal Ge2e4,Gf1g2] A pawn sacrifice: Qe2 prepares Bg2 against the queen on e4, and the greedy 7...Qxh1
8.Bg2 Qxh2 keeps Black's queen alive. The elite results are very good for White (36 wins in 54 games), but
this is the one line in the chapter for which I found no published explanation, so learn it only if you
want to risk it. } 7... Nf6 8. Bg2 )
5. e5 Nfd7 6. f4 c5 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Nf3 { **Verdict:** the same Steinitz position as in the previous line.
Prefer 4.Nc3 unless you like the gambit. } *
